From b56d6a374c019e4f447ac1170951ae224e4360ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 12:34:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07987/11362] [outer-loop] budget sweep: FIX the inverted
 scoring conclusion, the test unit, and the memory envelope

Applies six adversarial-review findings against the .528 budget-sweep artifact. The fatal one
INVERTED the artifact's own recommendation, so an operator following the published report would
have LOWERED the action budget when the data say raise it.

FATAL -- THE SCORING CONCLUSION WAS INVERTED, AND IT WAS BAKED INTO honest_verdict.
The analyser claimed the gateway's charging rule was "NOT resolvable locally" and reported two
models that "DISAGREE IN SIGN" (x0.18 at b2000), stamping
`scored_efficiency_term_degrades_quadratically`. All three parts were wrong, and CONFIRMED so by
reading + driving the installed scorer:
  * arc_agi.scorecard is INSTALLED in the analysis venv. Its card->score path (scorecard.py:474-491)
    DIFFERENCES successive level checkpoints and assigns the tail to the first INCOMPLETE level --
    byte-for-byte what arc_leaderboard_eval.run_game already drives.
  * MEASURED: the same solve scores 2.7778 with a 15-action tail and with a 100,000-action tail.
    The tail is score-FREE. The losing model contradicted the shipped code, it did not compete
    with it.
  * The model recovered a human baseline by inverting min(human/agent,1)^2 -- the formula a
    2026-06-20 review had ALREADY retracted. It also omitted `to_score`'s max_score clamp entirely.
The efficiency axis is now derived SOLELY from the authoritative scorer, which the analyser DRIVES
(`_resolve_charging_rule`, `_max_score_clamp_table`) rather than paraphrases. Restated headline:
score sum 9.0557 -> 9.2370 (x1.02) while won cells go 11 -> 36 (x3.27), i.e. the win count
overstates the leaderboard benefit ~150x; the 25 newly-won cells contribute 0.1789 total
(mean 0.0072) because they win at atfl 415-2715 against human baselines of tens.

A CORRECTION TO THE REVIEW ITSELF, found by writing the clamp's regression test: the clamp is a
CEILING (`min`), so "more levels is the dominant lever" holds only at EQUAL per-level speed. A
4-of-8 solve at 400/900/1500/2200 actions scores 0.1207 -- BELOW a 1-of-8 solve in 15 (2.7778).
Grinding is exactly what a raised budget buys, which is the mechanism behind the +2%.

PSEUDO-REPLICATION: the headline sign test is now on the GAME. 3 seeds of a game share its adapter,
mechanics and win condition; the inferential target is a hidden game. Game-level p by step:
0.5 / 0.0156 / 0.25 / 0.5 -- only ONE of four steps survives, against cell-level
0.0625 / 6.1e-5 / 0.0156 / 0.25. The cell-level test is retained, renamed as within-game
replicates. The module docstring already CLAIMED the game unit while the code counted cells; fixed.
EFFECT SIZE is untouched: +7 median games b400->b2000, zero regressions in 75/75 cells.

MEMORY IS THE BINDING CONSTRAINT, AND IT IS NOW MEASURED, NOT ESTIMATED. Swarm.main()
(agents/swarm.py:76-99) starts one thread per game before joining any, so retained graphs are
concurrent. New `scripts/arc_budget_memory_probe.py`: one clean process per (game, budget), every
lazily-imported module imported EAGERLY first so the baseline is the SHARED term. 24 cells, 6
games. shared_libs 813 MiB; per-game worst 55.8 / 87.0 / 144.3 / 259.2 MiB at b400/1000/2000/4000.
Projected to 110 games: 6.79 / 10.14 / 16.29 / 28.64 GiB. Largest budget that FITS at worst case
is b1000 -- NOT the b4000 the prior report treated as saturating. (A first attempt read 846 MiB at
b400; that snapshot preceded the harness's lazy numpy/arcengine/env imports and was almost entirely
SHARED cost. Fixed before use.) The 16 GiB host figure is a VRAM number from the requirements note
and is labelled UNCONFIRMED.

LLM-ON WALL CLOCK: the fixed-vs-proportional BAND is replaced by a mechanistic model, because the
identifying variable was already on every row. LLM cost = inductions x s_per_induction, and
measured inductions/game grow only 1.147 -> 1.613 (1.41x) over a 10x budget raise -- so the band's
1.0x lower bound was optimistic and its ~10x upper bound wildly pessimistic. Calibrated to the
measured b400 anchor (227.3 s/game, factor 1.220): b4000 = 343.5 s/game = 89.5% of the code-VERIFIED
12h envelope at 110 games, but 135.8% of the 8h preview envelope, which is already exceeded above
b400. `wall_clock_never_binding` is struck; the replacement scopes the claim to LLM-OFF.

honest_verdict no longer contradicts the artifact's own numbers: it states the measured score RISE,
scopes the wall-clock claim, and names memory as first-binding.

lever2 INSTRUMENTATION GAP CLOSED IN THE PUBLISHED RECORD. The lever-A/B artifact predated its own
analyser by 42 minutes and still carried hud_mask_resolved=None on 55/55 scored rows. Rebuilt with
the committed analyser: 805/805 rows now readable at the flat address via the nested back-fill (no
GPU cell re-run, no fabricated value). Top-level structure, all gates and honest_verdict are
BYTE-IDENTICAL. The HUD verdict remains UNINTERPRETABLE_EMPTY_PASS_REGION, so the "12/12 matched
cells favour keeping it" directional claim is WITHDRAWN, not asserted -- no committed artifact
supports it.

Also: arc_leaderboard_eval.py's header docstring still advertised the retracted formula while the
code below it had been correct since 2026-06-20. That stale line is what the analyser read as the
definition. Corrected, with the retraction recorded inline.

Spec: REQ-ARC-WMTE-5981 + 6 scenarios. 16 new regression tests, each against the counterexample
that motivated it; mutation-proved (charge model, one-sided test, dropped clamp, renamed key all
caught). NOTHING FLIPPED: MAX_ACTIONS stays 400/200, no SUBMITTED_* touched, no submission -- the
budget decision is the operator's, and the memory ceiling says cap it at b1000, not b4000.

